WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.820
Hi, I'm Nei Maldaner,

2
00:00:01.820 --> 00:00:04.120
and this is module five of OSWork.

3
00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:06.040
In modules three and four you set

4
00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:07.740
up the training folder and the config

5
00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:10.100
folder, with support files.

6
00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:12.140
Now those folders get instructions the agent

7
00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:13.100
follows.

8
00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:13.813
In this module,

9
00:00:13.813 --> 00:00:15.500
you'll write rules you can check in

10
00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:17.460
the delivery, separate what applies to every

11
00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:18.920
project from what applies to a single

12
00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:20.060
folder,

13
00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:22.640
turn a repeated step-by-step into a Skill,

14
00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:23.300
keep a short,

15
00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:26.620
dated memory, record failures as small protections,

16
00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:27.900
and test what you built with three

17
00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:28.400
cases.

18
00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:30.320
We'll keep going with Denise,

19
00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:32.120
the pedagogical coordinator,

20
00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:34.080
and Lúcia, the 8th grade science teacher.

21
00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:35.760
Each lesson has a short practice.

22
00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:36.780
When I ask,

23
00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:38.240
pause the video and do it right

24
00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:40.100
away, on your computer.

25
00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:41.740
Think about the science lab.

26
00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:43.840
On the door there's a sign that

27
00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:45.700
says how to work inside.

28
00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:46.900
AGENTS.md

29
00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:48.700
does the same for a project folder.

30
00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:50.300
It's a simple text file,

31
00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:52.720
in Markdown, that you open and edit

32
00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:54.480
in a text editor.

33
00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:56.040
And Codex looks for that file on

34
00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:57.700
its own when it starts working in

35
00:00:57.700 --> 00:00:58.740
the folder.

36
00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:00.820
Lúcia already has the training folder,

37
00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:01.960
meu primeiro projeto.

38
00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:03.920
Inside it are the README,

39
00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:06.180
the AGENTS.md, the entradas folder,

40
00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:08.080
with a fictional sales spreadsheet,

41
00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:09.480
and the saidas folder,

42
00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:12.060
where the agent delivers the drafts.

43
00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:13.660
AGENTS.md

44
00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:15.440
is the first file she's going to

45
00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:15.940
improve.

46
00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:17.940
If you didn't set up that folder,

47
00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:20.160
no problem: in your personal folder,

48
00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:23.440
create projetos, inside it meu primeiro projeto,

49
00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:25.050
and an empty

50
00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:25.840
AGENTS.md.

51
00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:27.380
What goes in this file?

52
00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:30.080
Four topics: where to start reading,

53
00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:32.140
how to check the result,

54
00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:32.900
what not to do,

55
00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:35.120
and the delivery format.

56
00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:37.220
The school's history and the reasons behind

57
00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:39.800
each decision stay out.

58
00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:41.520
A short file gets read in full.

59
00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:43.960
A long file hides

60
00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:45.800
the rule that matters in the middle

61
00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:46.440
of paragraphs.

62
00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:47.340
Denise

63
00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.420
opened the AGENTS.md

64
00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:51.420
she had written for the meeting minutes

65
00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:52.400
project.

66
00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:54.600
It had two paragraphs about the school's

67
00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:56.840
founding and only one work rule.

68
00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:59.300
She deleted the paragraphs and kept sentences

69
00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:00.500
like

70
00:02:00.851 --> 00:02:01.761
these:

71
00:02:01.761 --> 00:02:04.061
read the README before making changes,

72
00:02:04.061 --> 00:02:07.061
use only the entradas and saidas folders,

73
00:02:07.061 --> 00:02:08.601
report what you changed and how you

74
00:02:08.601 --> 00:02:09.681
checked it.

75
00:02:09.681 --> 00:02:12.721
Those sentences change what the agent does.

76
00:02:12.721 --> 00:02:14.661
The paragraphs about the founding just took

77
00:02:14.661 --> 00:02:16.021
up space.

78
00:02:16.241 --> 00:02:18.361
Now, the sign test.

79
00:02:18.361 --> 00:02:20.381
Wear safety goggles before the experiment is

80
00:02:20.381 --> 00:02:22.341
something you can check by looking.

81
00:02:22.341 --> 00:02:24.021
Be careful isn't.

82
00:02:24.021 --> 00:02:26.101
The same goes for AGENTS.md:

83
00:02:27.681 --> 00:02:29.401
a good rule asks for an action

84
00:02:29.401 --> 00:02:30.381
and

85
00:02:30.381 --> 00:02:32.041
leaves evidence you can see in the

86
00:02:32.041 --> 00:02:33.241
delivery.

87
00:02:33.681 --> 00:02:37.321
Lúcia replaced be excellent with compare

88
00:02:37.321 --> 00:02:39.841
the report total with the spreadsheet and

89
00:02:39.841 --> 00:02:41.001
state the difference.

90
00:02:41.001 --> 00:02:42.461
In the next delivery,

91
00:02:42.461 --> 00:02:44.341
the agent wrote the sum and the

92
00:02:44.341 --> 00:02:45.341
difference, and

93
00:02:45.341 --> 00:02:47.781
she checked it in one minute.

94
00:02:48.741 --> 00:02:50.121
Start with five rules,

95
00:02:50.121 --> 00:02:51.721
like the ones on screen.

96
00:02:51.721 --> 00:02:53.081
If you get stuck,

97
00:02:53.081 --> 00:02:54.701
use the test sentence:

98
00:02:54.701 --> 00:02:56.561
I'll know it was followed because the

99
00:02:56.561 --> 00:02:59.021
delivery shows such and such.

100
00:02:59.021 --> 00:03:00.281
Your practice:

101
00:03:00.281 --> 00:03:01.201
pause the video,

102
00:03:01.201 --> 00:03:03.641
open the AGENTS.md in the training folder,

103
00:03:03.641 --> 00:03:06.241
and write the purpose and five rules,

104
00:03:06.241 --> 00:03:08.801
each with its evidence next to it.

105
00:03:08.801 --> 00:03:10.361
It takes about ten minutes.

106
00:03:10.361 --> 00:03:11.841
The rules from the last lesson apply

107
00:03:11.841 --> 00:03:13.321
only in that folder.

108
00:03:13.321 --> 00:03:14.541
What about the ones you want in

109
00:03:14.541 --> 00:03:15.981
every project?

110
00:03:16.561 --> 00:03:17.921
Think about school:

111
00:03:17.921 --> 00:03:19.781
the rule book applies to the whole

112
00:03:19.781 --> 00:03:21.921
building, and the class agreement applies in

113
00:03:21.921 --> 00:03:23.241
the classroom.

114
00:03:23.441 --> 00:03:25.961
AGENTS.md works the same way.

115
00:03:25.961 --> 00:03:28.081
By default, the global one lives in

116
00:03:28.081 --> 00:03:30.321
a hidden folder called dot codex,

117
00:03:30.321 --> 00:03:32.721
inside your personal folder.

118
00:03:32.721 --> 00:03:34.481
The project one lives in the project

119
00:03:34.481 --> 00:03:35.281
folder.

120
00:03:35.281 --> 00:03:36.241
In the terminal,

121
00:03:36.241 --> 00:03:37.681
the tilde sign is the shortcut to

122
00:03:37.681 --> 00:03:39.161
your personal folder,

123
00:03:39.161 --> 00:03:40.901
and a folder whose name starts with

124
00:03:40.901 --> 00:03:42.781
a dot is hidden.

125
00:03:42.921 --> 00:03:44.781
Denise wants the agent to always say

126
00:03:44.781 --> 00:03:45.481
what it checked,

127
00:03:45.481 --> 00:03:46.961
in any project.

128
00:03:46.961 --> 00:03:49.361
That rule went into the global file.

129
00:03:49.361 --> 00:03:51.181
But compare the total with the attendance

130
00:03:51.181 --> 00:03:53.601
spreadsheet only makes sense in the attendance

131
00:03:53.601 --> 00:03:54.801
project.

132
00:03:55.141 --> 00:03:56.301
The two work together,

133
00:03:56.301 --> 00:03:58.761
and the global one stays small.

134
00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:01.600
A subfolder can have its own

135
00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:02.800
AGENTS.md.

136
00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:05.340
With Codex open in it,

137
00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:08.080
both apply, and the closest instruction takes

138
00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:10.220
precedence on what it covers.

139
00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:12.420
Whatever it doesn't mention still comes from

140
00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:13.620
the file above.

141
00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:15.480
Lúcia created a provas subfolder,

142
00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:18.540
for tests, with the rule answer key

143
00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:19.960
in a separate file.

144
00:04:19.960 --> 00:04:20.900
Opened in provas,

145
00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:21.960
it applies.

146
00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:23.300
In the exercise lists,

147
00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:24.540
it doesn't.

148
00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:26.280
There's also AGENTS.override:

149
00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:28.500
in the same folder,

150
00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:31.020
Codex reads the override and ignores

151
00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:32.000
AGENTS.md.

152
00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:33.780
Forgotten in a copied folder,

153
00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:35.900
it explains a lot of strange behavior.

154
00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:37.620
And none of these files beats three

155
00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:38.820
things:

156
00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:40.680
the system instructions,

157
00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:41.960
the permissions,

158
00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:44.720
and your explicit request in the conversation.

159
00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:47.700
Denise's file says deliver in bullet points,

160
00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:49.220
but today she asked for a continuous

161
00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:50.320
paragraph.

162
00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:51.800
Today's request wins.

163
00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:53.540
How do you know which instructions apply

164
00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:54.920
in a folder?

165
00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:56.900
Ask Codex to summarize the instructions it

166
00:04:56.900 --> 00:04:58.980
loaded and say which file each one

167
00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:00.020
came from.

168
00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:01.860
Then open the files and compare.

169
00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:02.641
Lúcia did that,

170
00:05:02.641 --> 00:05:05.001
and the rule report what you checked

171
00:05:05.001 --> 00:05:07.941
showed up, which she hadn't written there.

172
00:05:07.941 --> 00:05:09.601
It was in the global file,

173
00:05:09.601 --> 00:05:11.441
created in March and forgotten.

174
00:05:11.441 --> 00:05:13.441
Your practice, about ten minutes:

175
00:05:13.441 --> 00:05:14.521
pause the video.

176
00:05:14.521 --> 00:05:16.001
In the terminal,

177
00:05:16.001 --> 00:05:18.381
go into the training folder and run

178
00:05:18.381 --> 00:05:19.701
ls dash a,

179
00:05:19.701 --> 00:05:21.781
which also shows hidden files,

180
00:05:21.781 --> 00:05:24.711
and write down whether there's an override.

181
00:05:24.711 --> 00:05:27.161
Then run cat on the global

182
00:05:27.161 --> 00:05:27.901
AGENTS.md.

183
00:05:27.901 --> 00:05:29.821
If it says the file doesn't exist,

184
00:05:29.821 --> 00:05:31.641
you don't have a global file,

185
00:05:31.761 --> 00:05:32.981
and that's fine.

186
00:05:32.981 --> 00:05:35.021
Finally, open Codex,

187
00:05:35.021 --> 00:05:36.921
ask for the summary saying don't change

188
00:05:36.921 --> 00:05:39.321
anything, and compare each rule with its

189
00:05:39.321 --> 00:05:40.601
source file.

190
00:05:40.601 --> 00:05:42.481
A rule says what to respect.

191
00:05:42.481 --> 00:05:44.101
What about the procedure you repeat every

192
00:05:44.101 --> 00:05:46.261
week, step by step?

193
00:05:46.641 --> 00:05:48.141
Back to the lab.

194
00:05:48.141 --> 00:05:50.241
The sign on the door applies every

195
00:05:50.241 --> 00:05:51.121
day.

196
00:05:51.121 --> 00:05:52.861
The experiment sheet comes out of the

197
00:05:52.861 --> 00:05:54.621
binder only on the day of that

198
00:05:54.621 --> 00:05:55.821
experiment.

199
00:05:56.021 --> 00:05:57.221
AGENTS.md

200
00:05:57.541 --> 00:05:58.601
is the sign.

201
00:05:58.601 --> 00:05:59.921
The Skill is the sheet.

202
00:05:59.921 --> 00:06:02.461
A Skill gathers the instructions for an

203
00:06:02.461 --> 00:06:03.121
activity that repeats,

204
00:06:03.121 --> 00:06:05.721
and the agent triggers it when the

205
00:06:05.721 --> 00:06:07.383
task calls for it.

206
00:06:07.383 --> 00:06:09.203
Every Friday, Lúcia explained to the agent

207
00:06:09.203 --> 00:06:11.103
how to put together the weekly exercise

208
00:06:11.103 --> 00:06:12.303
list:

209
00:06:12.823 --> 00:06:14.483
read that week's lesson,

210
00:06:14.483 --> 00:06:16.043
choose five questions,

211
00:06:16.043 --> 00:06:17.923
keep the answer key separate.

212
00:06:17.923 --> 00:06:19.723
That step-by-step became a Skill,

213
00:06:19.723 --> 00:06:21.283
and AGENTS.md

214
00:06:21.283 --> 00:06:23.023
kept its five rules.

215
00:06:23.023 --> 00:06:24.443
Both are

216
00:06:24.563 --> 00:06:27.103
right, each in its role.

217
00:06:27.103 --> 00:06:29.603
Separating them avoids loading the whole manual

218
00:06:29.603 --> 00:06:31.363
into every task.

219
00:06:31.363 --> 00:06:33.063
A Skill lives in a folder with

220
00:06:33.063 --> 00:06:36.083
its name, in a file called SKILL.md.

221
00:06:36.843 --> 00:06:37.343
At the top,

222
00:06:37.303 --> 00:06:39.183
between two lines of three dashes,

223
00:06:39.183 --> 00:06:42.043
come the name and the description.

224
00:06:42.523 --> 00:06:44.963
That block is the header.

225
00:06:44.963 --> 00:06:46.823
After it comes the procedure:

226
00:06:46.823 --> 00:06:47.903
what goes in,

227
00:06:47.903 --> 00:06:49.863
the steps, what comes out,

228
00:06:49.863 --> 00:06:50.983
and how to check.

229
00:06:50.983 --> 00:06:53.003
The description is the most important part,

230
00:06:53.003 --> 00:06:56.063
because it's what the agent reads to

231
00:06:56.263 --> 00:06:57.843
decide whether to trigger the Skill.

232
00:06:57.843 --> 00:06:59.943
It needs to say in which situation

233
00:06:59.943 --> 00:07:01.643
to use it and where the Skill

234
00:07:01.643 --> 00:07:02.483
stops.

235
00:07:02.483 --> 00:07:04.663
The first description of Denise's report Skill

236
00:07:04.663 --> 00:07:06.423
was help with reports.

237
00:07:06.423 --> 00:07:07.543
The

238
00:07:07.543 --> 00:07:09.363
agent triggered it even for a meeting

239
00:07:09.363 --> 00:07:10.624
minutes request.

240
00:07:10.624 --> 00:07:12.264
She rewrote it:

241
00:07:12.324 --> 00:07:14.464
generate a weekly report draft when the

242
00:07:14.464 --> 00:07:15.824
user provides a sales CSV,

243
00:07:15.824 --> 00:07:18.084
and don't use it to send reports

244
00:07:18.084 --> 00:07:19.404
or handle credentials,

245
00:07:19.404 --> 00:07:21.244
which are passwords and access keys.

246
00:07:21.244 --> 00:07:22.544
Where do you keep it?

247
00:07:22.544 --> 00:07:25.844
A project Skill goes in dot agents

248
00:07:25.844 --> 00:07:26.764
slash

249
00:07:26.764 --> 00:07:29.704
skills, inside the project folder.

250
00:07:29.704 --> 00:07:31.064
A personal Skill,

251
00:07:31.064 --> 00:07:32.244
one you want in every project,

252
00:07:32.244 --> 00:07:33.624
goes in that same folder,

253
00:07:33.624 --> 00:07:35.469
but inside your personal folder.

254
00:07:35.469 --> 00:07:37.524
The dot codex folder belongs to Codex,

255
00:07:37.524 --> 00:07:40.524
with the configuration and the global file.

256
00:07:40.524 --> 00:07:42.084
Skills don't go there.

257
00:07:42.084 --> 00:07:44.764
Denise kept the meeting minutes Skill in

258
00:07:44.764 --> 00:07:46.284
the minutes project,

259
00:07:46.284 --> 00:07:47.924
and the spelling review one,

260
00:07:47.924 --> 00:07:49.784
which she uses for everything,

261
00:07:49.784 --> 00:07:51.124
in her personal folder.

262
00:07:51.124 --> 00:07:53.664
Your practice, about ten minutes:

263
00:07:53.664 --> 00:07:54.464
pause the video.

264
00:07:54.464 --> 00:07:56.704
Copy the template from the lesson and

265
00:07:56.704 --> 00:07:58.864
save it as SKILL.md in the training

266
00:07:58.864 --> 00:08:00.804
folder, with the file type set to

267
00:08:00.804 --> 00:08:01.564
all files.

268
00:08:01.564 --> 00:08:02.564
In the terminal,

269
00:08:02.564 --> 00:08:05.259
create the relatorio semanal folder with

270
00:08:05.259 --> 00:08:06.504
mkdir dash p,

271
00:08:06.504 --> 00:08:09.084
move the file into it with mv,

272
00:08:09.084 --> 00:08:11.444
and run head dash four.

273
00:08:11.444 --> 00:08:13.324
You're done when the four header lines

274
00:08:13.324 --> 00:08:13.924
appear.

275
00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:16.000
A Skill doesn't remember what you agreed

276
00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:17.380
on yesterday.

277
00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:19.140
So where do you keep what needs

278
00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:20.660
to stay valid?

279
00:08:20.660 --> 00:08:22.680
First, a clarification:

280
00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:24.400
the model doesn't change because you talked

281
00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:25.280
to it.

282
00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:27.160
The operational memory in this course is

283
00:08:27.160 --> 00:08:29.040
a set of files the agent reads

284
00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:30.740
when you point to them.

285
00:08:30.740 --> 00:08:32.700
It works under two conditions:

286
00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:34.980
the agent reads the part that matters,

287
00:08:34.980 --> 00:08:36.440
and someone keeps the file up to

288
00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:37.120
date.

289
00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:38.360
In module four,

290
00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:39.880
Denise created the config folder,

291
00:08:39.880 --> 00:08:40.800
next

292
00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:42.040
to the projects.

293
00:08:42.040 --> 00:08:43.700
That's where memoria lives,

294
00:08:43.700 --> 00:08:46.500
with stable facts and preferences,

295
00:08:46.900 --> 00:08:48.900
decisoes, with the decisions made and the

296
00:08:48.900 --> 00:08:50.260
reason, and falhas,

297
00:08:50.260 --> 00:08:51.960
with the causes of failures,

298
00:08:51.960 --> 00:08:53.280
which is the topic of the next

299
00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:53.780
lesson.

300
00:08:53.420 --> 00:08:55.440
If you skipped that part,

301
00:08:55.440 --> 00:08:58.207
create the config folder inside projetos and,

302
00:08:58.207 --> 00:08:59.740
in it, a

303
00:08:59.920 --> 00:09:01.900
file called memoria.md.

304
00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:05.140
What goes into memory?

305
00:09:05.140 --> 00:09:07.620
Three things: stable facts,

306
00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:08.820
decisions,

307
00:09:08.820 --> 00:09:10.590
and causes of failures.

308
00:09:10.590 --> 00:09:13.540
Not every sentence from every conversation.

309
00:09:13.540 --> 00:09:16.303
Copying the whole history adds volume and

310
00:09:16.303 --> 00:09:18.663
can bring back an old instruction,

311
00:09:18.663 --> 00:09:20.863
and nobody can check a huge file.

312
00:09:20.863 --> 00:09:22.343
Look at Lúcia's case.

313
00:09:22.343 --> 00:09:23.923
She had two hundred lines of conversation,

314
00:09:23.923 --> 00:09:25.963
from March to September,

315
00:09:25.963 --> 00:09:27.523
and in the middle a sentence asking

316
00:09:27.523 --> 00:09:29.063
for the old list format,

317
00:09:29.063 --> 00:09:30.423
which had already changed.

318
00:09:30.423 --> 00:09:31.803
She replaced all of it with three

319
00:09:31.803 --> 00:09:32.743
lines:

320
00:09:32.743 --> 00:09:33.923
the review date,

321
00:09:33.923 --> 00:09:36.143
8th grade materials with accessible language and

322
00:09:36.143 --> 00:09:37.663
fictional examples,

323
00:09:37.663 --> 00:09:39.603
and a weekly list with five questions

324
00:09:39.603 --> 00:09:41.323
and a separate answer key.

325
00:09:41.323 --> 00:09:43.923
From two hundred lines to three,

326
00:09:44.083 --> 00:09:46.143
and no outdated instructions.

327
00:09:46.203 --> 00:09:47.343
In the next task,

328
00:09:47.343 --> 00:09:49.063
she pointed to that file instead of

329
00:09:49.063 --> 00:09:50.723
repeating the conversation.

330
00:09:50.723 --> 00:09:52.283
The file being in the folder doesn't

331
00:09:52.283 --> 00:09:53.703
guarantee the agent will read it.

332
00:09:53.703 --> 00:09:55.623
Say in your request which file to

333
00:09:55.623 --> 00:09:57.503
check, and ask it to cite the

334
00:09:57.503 --> 00:09:58.823
fact it used.

335
00:09:58.823 --> 00:10:00.763
Denise asked for a three-line reminder about

336
00:10:00.763 --> 00:10:01.883
the parent meeting,

337
00:10:01.883 --> 00:10:02.823
pointing to the memory.

338
00:10:02.823 --> 00:10:04.783
The answer ended with the fact it

339
00:10:04.783 --> 00:10:07.123
used: reminders to families of up to

340
00:10:07.123 --> 00:10:09.523
three lines, with no abbreviations.

341
00:10:09.743 --> 00:10:11.703
She checked it in the file.

342
00:10:11.703 --> 00:10:13.643
And stable facts change too:

343
00:10:13.643 --> 00:10:15.645
mark the review date at the top

344
00:10:15.645 --> 00:10:17.665
and note the source of each fact.

345
00:10:17.665 --> 00:10:18.985
In September,

346
00:10:19.705 --> 00:10:22.005
Denise deleted reminders go home printed in

347
00:10:22.005 --> 00:10:24.285
the backpack, because the school switched to

348
00:10:24.285 --> 00:10:25.465
the app.

349
00:10:25.465 --> 00:10:26.745
Your practice:

350
00:10:26.745 --> 00:10:29.225
pause, write the date and three facts

351
00:10:29.225 --> 00:10:30.465
with their source,

352
00:10:30.465 --> 00:10:32.905
no student names and no passwords.

353
00:10:32.905 --> 00:10:35.565
Then, in Codex or your chat,

354
00:10:35.685 --> 00:10:37.205
ask for a short task pointing to

355
00:10:37.205 --> 00:10:39.005
the file and check the fact it

356
00:10:39.005 --> 00:10:40.005
cites.

357
00:10:40.005 --> 00:10:41.565
When something goes wrong,

358
00:10:41.565 --> 00:10:43.345
what goes into the record?

359
00:10:43.345 --> 00:10:45.385
Start by separating two things.

360
00:10:45.385 --> 00:10:47.125
The report came out empty is the

361
00:10:47.125 --> 00:10:48.445
symptom, what you saw.

362
00:10:48.445 --> 00:10:50.865
The cause is the reason you observed,

363
00:10:50.865 --> 00:10:53.845
like the spreadsheet had no records.

364
00:10:53.845 --> 00:10:56.665
Notice: observed, not assumed.

365
00:10:56.665 --> 00:10:57.985
In the training project,

366
00:10:57.985 --> 00:10:59.985
Lúcia saw the report come out empty.

367
00:10:59.985 --> 00:11:01.285
Before touching anything,

368
00:11:01.285 --> 00:11:03.085
she wrote down the symptom and opened

369
00:11:03.085 --> 00:11:04.165
the input spreadsheet.

370
00:11:04.165 --> 00:11:05.825
It had no records at all.

371
00:11:05.825 --> 00:11:08.525
After the symptom and the cause come

372
00:11:08.525 --> 00:11:10.125
two more columns:

373
00:11:10.765 --> 00:11:11.985
the smallest fix,

374
00:11:11.985 --> 00:11:13.665
which here is checking the number of

375
00:11:13.665 --> 00:11:15.105
rows before generating,

376
00:11:15.105 --> 00:11:16.945
and the verification,

377
00:11:16.945 --> 00:11:18.668
which says how you know the fix

378
00:11:18.668 --> 00:11:22.068
works: an empty spreadsheet produces a warning,

379
00:11:22.068 --> 00:11:22.848
not a report.

380
00:11:22.848 --> 00:11:25.108
All in a single table row,

381
00:11:25.108 --> 00:11:26.528
in the falhas file,

382
00:11:26.528 --> 00:11:28.328
with the date and the type.

383
00:11:28.328 --> 00:11:29.928
And what type of failure is it?

384
00:11:29.928 --> 00:11:32.008
A request failure is when the goal

385
00:11:32.008 --> 00:11:34.328
was ambiguous or information was missing.

386
00:11:34.328 --> 00:11:36.948
An infrastructure failure is when the text

387
00:11:36.948 --> 00:11:39.978
was fine, but something outside it failed,

388
00:11:39.978 --> 00:11:41.748
like a missing file or a process

389
00:11:41.748 --> 00:11:43.088
that stopped.

390
00:11:43.088 --> 00:11:44.928
Denise had one of each in the

391
00:11:44.928 --> 00:11:45.428
same week.

392
00:11:45.368 --> 00:11:48.048
The minutes summary came out two pages

393
00:11:48.048 --> 00:11:50.268
long, because the request didn't say the

394
00:11:50.268 --> 00:11:50.768
length.

395
00:11:50.608 --> 00:11:52.608
The fix was in the text:

396
00:11:52.608 --> 00:11:53.848
up to ten lines.

397
00:11:53.848 --> 00:11:56.848
The attendance report didn't come out because

398
00:11:56.848 --> 00:11:58.608
the spreadsheet wasn't in the folder.

399
00:11:58.608 --> 00:12:01.168
The fix was a check:

400
00:12:01.228 --> 00:12:04.308
confirm the file exists before starting.

401
00:12:04.308 --> 00:12:05.948
When in doubt,

402
00:12:05.948 --> 00:12:07.148
ask:

403
00:12:07.168 --> 00:12:08.428
if I had written it better,

404
00:12:08.428 --> 00:12:09.608
would it have worked?

405
00:12:09.608 --> 00:12:11.588
If yes, it was the request.

406
00:12:11.588 --> 00:12:13.388
If it was both,

407
00:12:13.388 --> 00:12:14.968
mark both.

408
00:12:15.428 --> 00:12:17.728
And prefer the smallest fix to a

409
00:12:17.728 --> 00:12:18.293
rebuild.

410
00:12:18.620 --> 00:12:20.360
Faced with the empty report,

411
00:12:20.360 --> 00:12:22.340
Lúcia thought about switching models.

412
00:12:22.340 --> 00:12:24.020
That would take two hours,

413
00:12:24.020 --> 00:12:25.780
and the empty spreadsheet would keep breaking

414
00:12:25.780 --> 00:12:27.260
the next report.

415
00:12:27.260 --> 00:12:28.900
One new line was enough.

416
00:12:28.900 --> 00:12:30.780
And that line goes into

417
00:12:30.780 --> 00:12:31.980
AGENTS.md

418
00:12:33.180 --> 00:12:34.740
or the Skill,

419
00:12:34.740 --> 00:12:37.580
which the agent reads on every task.

420
00:12:37.720 --> 00:12:39.800
Denise wrote in the attendance one:

421
00:12:39.800 --> 00:12:42.760
before reading, check that the spreadsheet exists.

422
00:12:42.900 --> 00:12:43.660
If it's missing,

423
00:12:43.660 --> 00:12:45.680
stop and say which file is missing.

424
00:12:45.680 --> 00:12:46.780
The following week,

425
00:12:46.780 --> 00:12:48.920
the agent stopped and warned her.

426
00:12:48.920 --> 00:12:50.920
Your practice, about twelve minutes:

427
00:12:50.920 --> 00:12:52.120
pause.

428
00:12:52.280 --> 00:12:53.260
In the terminal,

429
00:12:53.260 --> 00:12:55.100
list the entradas folder and look for

430
00:12:55.100 --> 00:12:57.580
an October file that doesn't exist.

431
00:12:57.580 --> 00:12:59.640
Record that fictional failure in falhas,

432
00:12:59.640 --> 00:13:01.640
paste the rule into AGENTS.md,

433
00:13:01.640 --> 00:13:03.920
open Codex, and ask for the October

434
00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:05.120
report.

435
00:13:05.320 --> 00:13:06.540
Check that it stops,

436
00:13:06.540 --> 00:13:07.620
says which file is missing,

437
00:13:07.620 --> 00:13:09.780
and doesn't make up numbers.

438
00:13:09.780 --> 00:13:11.040
The Skill worked once.

439
00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:11.660
Does it always work?

440
00:13:11.660 --> 00:13:13.480
To find out,

441
00:13:13.480 --> 00:13:15.740
you'll test it with three cases.

442
00:13:15.740 --> 00:13:17.060
And before running it,

443
00:13:17.060 --> 00:13:18.860
you write on a checklist what each

444
00:13:18.860 --> 00:13:20.600
case should produce.

445
00:13:20.600 --> 00:13:22.280
That way you compare with a reference,

446
00:13:22.280 --> 00:13:24.297
not with your impression in the moment.

447
00:13:24.297 --> 00:13:26.397
Denise set up the checklist the way

448
00:13:26.397 --> 00:13:28.637
she sets up the evacuation drill sheet:

449
00:13:28.637 --> 00:13:30.657
first what should happen,

450
00:13:30.657 --> 00:13:32.077
then the run-through.

451
00:13:32.077 --> 00:13:33.177
In the normal case,

452
00:13:33.177 --> 00:13:35.344
the spreadsheet has a notebook at thirty-five

453
00:13:35.344 --> 00:13:37.157
fifty, a pen at nine fifty,

454
00:13:37.157 --> 00:13:38.497
and a planner at fifty-five,

455
00:13:38.497 --> 00:13:40.697
and the expected total is one hundred.

456
00:13:40.697 --> 00:13:42.517
In the incomplete case,

457
00:13:42.517 --> 00:13:43.377
the pen has no value,

458
00:13:43.377 --> 00:13:45.597
so the expected result is the known

459
00:13:45.597 --> 00:13:47.697
total of ninety fifty and a pending

460
00:13:47.697 --> 00:13:48.457
item for the pen.

461
00:13:48.457 --> 00:13:50.557
In the not as agreed case,

462
00:13:50.557 --> 00:13:52.537
the request is generate and send to

463
00:13:52.537 --> 00:13:55.237
management, and the expected result is only

464
00:13:55.237 --> 00:13:57.397
the draft, with nothing sent.

465
00:13:57.397 --> 00:13:58.517
In the incomplete case,

466
00:13:58.517 --> 00:14:01.077
the right result doesn't invent the missing

467
00:14:01.077 --> 00:14:01.577
value.

468
00:14:01.537 --> 00:14:03.897
It shows the total of what's known

469
00:14:03.897 --> 00:14:06.257
and keeps the pending item in plain

470
00:14:06.257 --> 00:14:06.977
view.

471
00:14:06.977 --> 00:14:08.897
The first version of Lúcia's Skill was

472
00:14:08.897 --> 00:14:12.157
missing the rule don't invent missing values,

473
00:14:12.217 --> 00:14:14.317
and the agent answered with a total

474
00:14:14.317 --> 00:14:15.037
of one hundred,

475
00:14:15.037 --> 00:14:17.177
as if the pen had a value.

476
00:14:17.177 --> 00:14:19.897
It was the test that revealed this.

477
00:14:20.077 --> 00:14:21.417
After the fix,

478
00:14:21.417 --> 00:14:23.197
the answer brought the source,

479
00:14:23.197 --> 00:14:25.517
the draft in the saidas folder,

480
00:14:25.517 --> 00:14:27.255
the known total of ninety fifty,

481
00:14:27.255 --> 00:14:30.275
and the pen as a pending item,

482
00:14:30.295 --> 00:14:31.115
left blank.

483
00:14:31.115 --> 00:14:32.275
In the third case,

484
00:14:32.275 --> 00:14:34.935
Denise deliberately asked to send

485
00:14:34.935 --> 00:14:36.395
the report to management.

486
00:14:36.395 --> 00:14:38.115
Codex can't even send email there.

487
00:14:38.115 --> 00:14:39.975
The test checks whether it respects the

488
00:14:39.975 --> 00:14:42.215
limit: it made the draft,

489
00:14:42.215 --> 00:14:43.275
checked the total,

490
00:14:43.275 --> 00:14:44.795
and said it didn't send,

491
00:14:44.795 --> 00:14:46.955
because the Skill stops before sending or

492
00:14:46.955 --> 00:14:48.155
publishing.

493
00:14:48.155 --> 00:14:49.315
Looking for the Skill's name in the

494
00:14:49.315 --> 00:14:51.095
answer isn't a test.

495
00:14:51.095 --> 00:14:53.615
A test means answering three questions,

496
00:14:53.615 --> 00:14:55.475
looking at the answer and the report

497
00:14:55.475 --> 00:14:57.475
file in the saidas folder:

498
00:14:57.475 --> 00:14:59.495
does the total equal the sum?

499
00:14:59.495 --> 00:15:00.935
Is the pending item visible,

500
00:15:00.935 --> 00:15:02.375
with no made-up number?

501
00:15:02.375 --> 00:15:04.135
Did it stop before sending?

502
00:15:04.135 --> 00:15:05.455
If a case fails,

503
00:15:05.455 --> 00:15:07.235
add to the Skill the smallest rule

504
00:15:07.235 --> 00:15:09.235
that fixes that failure and run it

505
00:15:09.235 --> 00:15:10.435
again.

506
00:15:11.015 --> 00:15:13.215
Lúcia didn't rewrite the Skill.

507
00:15:13.215 --> 00:15:14.835
She added one line,

508
00:15:14.835 --> 00:15:17.335
an empty value becomes a pending item,

509
00:15:17.335 --> 00:15:18.815
never fill it in,

510
00:15:18.815 --> 00:15:20.275
and all three cases passed.

511
00:15:20.275 --> 00:15:21.535
Your practice,

512
00:15:21.535 --> 00:15:23.215
about twelve minutes:

513
00:15:23.835 --> 00:15:25.335
pause the video.

514
00:15:25.395 --> 00:15:28.235
Create the two fictional spreadsheets with the

515
00:15:28.235 --> 00:15:29.295
commands from the lesson.

516
00:15:29.295 --> 00:15:30.535
For each case,

517
00:15:30.535 --> 00:15:32.092
open Codex,

518
00:15:32.092 --> 00:15:33.332
send the request,

519
00:15:33.332 --> 00:15:35.752
exit, and read the report.

520
00:15:35.852 --> 00:15:39.052
Mark passed or failed on the checklist.

521
00:15:39.052 --> 00:15:40.252
If one fails,

522
00:15:40.252 --> 00:15:42.552
add only the smallest rule.

523
00:15:42.712 --> 00:15:44.432
Let's put it all together.

524
00:15:44.432 --> 00:15:46.512
You write instructions you can check in

525
00:15:46.512 --> 00:15:49.392
the delivery, with action and evidence,

526
00:15:49.392 --> 00:15:51.292
and you remove from the file whatever

527
00:15:51.292 --> 00:15:52.712
doesn't change the work.

528
00:15:52.712 --> 00:15:54.432
You keep the global file small and

529
00:15:54.432 --> 00:15:55.492
the details in the project,

530
00:15:55.492 --> 00:15:57.692
knowing that the closest file decides there

531
00:15:57.692 --> 00:16:00.372
and that your explicit request counts more.

532
00:16:00.372 --> 00:16:03.132
You turn the step-by-step that repeats into

533
00:16:03.132 --> 00:16:04.812
a Skill, with

534
00:16:04.952 --> 00:16:07.392
a description that says when to use

535
00:16:07.392 --> 00:16:08.732
it and when not to.

536
00:16:08.732 --> 00:16:11.032
You keep in memory only what stays

537
00:16:11.032 --> 00:16:13.052
valid, with date and source,

538
00:16:13.052 --> 00:16:15.392
and you point to the file in

539
00:16:15.392 --> 00:16:16.332
your request.

540
00:16:16.332 --> 00:16:18.392
You record failures as small protections,

541
00:16:18.392 --> 00:16:21.452
where the next run reads them.

542
00:16:21.992 --> 00:16:23.972
And you test the Skill with three

543
00:16:23.972 --> 00:16:25.872
cases before trusting it.

544
00:16:25.872 --> 00:16:28.192
When you have about thirty minutes,

545
00:16:28.192 --> 00:16:29.832
do the module lab in the supplementary

546
00:16:29.832 --> 00:16:31.572
material for lesson thirty.

547
00:16:31.572 --> 00:16:32.932
In module six:

548
00:16:32.932 --> 00:16:35.372
Git and GitHub without losing work.

549
00:16:35.372 --> 00:16:36.389
See you there.
